WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.713 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How can a large sharing problem become two smaller ones?

00:00:04.363 --> 00:00:07.590 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are dividing by a single-digit number.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:14.440 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will choose useful parts, share each part equally, and
use multiplication to check that no amount has been lost.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:20.648 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to divide whole numbers by a
single-digit number using useful parts.

00:00:21.298 --> 00:00:23.055 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:23.275 --> 00:00:25.948 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can choose parts that divide equally.

00:00:26.168 --> 00:00:29.316 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can divide every part by the same divisor.

00:00:29.536 --> 00:00:32.849 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can combine the quotients and multiply back to check.

00:00:33.499 --> 00:00:38.574 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Division can mean sharing into equal groups or finding how
many equal groups fit.

00:00:38.794 --> 00:00:41.172 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose parts that divide exactly.

00:00:41.392 --> 00:00:46.060 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The parts must add to the original dividend, and their
quotients must be combined.

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:49.560 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Picture twenty-four counters in four equal groups.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.355 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each group gets six, and every counter is shared once.

00:00:54.005 --> 00:00:57.825 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Divide one thousand five hundred and thirty-six by six.

00:00:58.045 --> 00:01:01.948 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Split it into one thousand five hundred, and a separate
thirty-six.

00:01:02.168 --> 00:01:06.654 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
One thousand five hundred divided by six is two hundred and
fifty.

00:01:07.304 --> 00:01:10.148 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Thirty-six divided by six is six.

00:01:10.368 --> 00:01:15.372 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Add the quotients: two hundred and fifty plus six gives two
hundred and fifty-six.

00:01:15.592 --> 00:01:19.303 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check by multiplying two hundred and fifty-six by six.

00:01:19.953 --> 00:01:23.417 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Divide two thousand one hundred and eighty-four by seven.

00:01:23.637 --> 00:01:27.545 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Two thousand one hundred divides into three hundred groups
of seven.

00:01:28.195 --> 00:01:31.126 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Eighty-four divided by seven is twelve.

00:01:31.346 --> 00:01:34.533 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Combine the quotients to get three hundred and twelve.

00:01:34.753 --> 00:01:38.522 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Multiplying back gives two thousand one hundred and
eighty-four.

00:01:39.172 --> 00:01:43.242 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
When you split a dividend, divide every part by the same
divisor.

00:01:43.462 --> 00:01:48.167 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Sharing only fifteen hundred and ignoring thirty-six loses
some of the amount.

00:01:48.387 --> 00:01:51.771 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Combine both quotients, then multiply back to check.

00:01:52.421 --> 00:01:55.897 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What is eight hundred and sixteen divided by four?

00:01:59.897 --> 00:02:05.095 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Eight hundred divided by four is two hundred, and sixteen
divided by four is four.

00:02:05.315 --> 00:02:07.888 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The quotient is two hundred and four.

00:02:08.538 --> 00:02:14.043 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Partition into useful parts, divide each part by the same
divisor, and add the quotients.

00:02:14.263 --> 00:02:19.180 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Check that quotient times divisor returns the dividend when
there is no remainder.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.748 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a model and explain why your strategy works.

00:02:22.968 --> 00:02:26.080 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now try the lesson practice on MathMagic!
